# ==============================================================================
# SCENARIO C: NO MESH YET -- GO AND MAKE ONE
# ==============================================================================
# mesh_scan.dpi takes a mesh and scans it. This is the stage in front of that:
# fly a sensor over the part, accumulate what it sees, and write a mesh file the
# planner and the projector can use. Feed the result to plan_mesh_path() and the
# rest of the pipeline does not know the difference.
#
# Two producers behind one interface, because the caller only wants a mesh file:
#
#   AzureReconstruction        RGB-D TSDF integration (industrial_reconstruction)
#                              over a dome sweep. Rough, fast, no laser.
#   KeyenceReconstructionKey   laser profiler -> PCL surface reconstruction
#                              (tari_key_reconstruction). High resolution, slow.
#   KeyenceReconstructionPC    laser profiler -> Open3D ball pivoting
#                              (tari_pc_reconstruction). Same sensor, different
#                              backend -- see KEYENCE_BACKEND in the config.
#
# Both Keyence backends ship. Which one a cell uses is a LOAD-TIME choice, not a
# runtime argument, because it decides which services have to be running and that
# has to be visible at bring-up rather than discovered when a scan calls it.
#
# The pattern worth noticing: the Keyence raster is itself a Noether toolpath
# over the rough mesh. So reconstruction is not a different kind of thing from
# scanning -- it is the same "plan a path over a surface, fly it, accumulate"
# loop with a different sensor and a different accumulator. That is why the sweep
# returns a ScanPath and is flown by the same code the UT scan uses.
#
# Deliberately free of any UT/eddy-current specifics and of paramdb: every
# parameter is an argument with a default, so this module can serve
# UTPlaneScan.dgp, ClientScannerTest.dgp and whatever comes next.
#
# Service calls run on their own short-lived node rather than through the dgpy
# motion module, exactly as noether_path.dpi does: a service call that blocks
# while holding a module context is the standard way to wedge this stack.

import contextlib
